PUBLIC RESPONSE: {After weighing the points raised so far, I continue to see the strongest support for N (I and III). Stab inoculation into 1 % agar is the standard, reproducible method for visualizing twitching zones at the agar–plastic interface, so statement I is solidly correct. Likewise, multiple published swarm-media recipes (including minimal media with 0.4–0.5 % agar) explicitly list glycerol as a carbon source that permits robust swarming in P. aeruginosa, confirming III.

Statement II is inconsistent with the thin-agar requirement; most twitching protocols specify 10–15 ml per 10-cm plate to keep the layer shallow enough for subsurface migration. On IV, while iron limitation can affect motility under some conditions, the literature shows considerable strain- and medium-dependent variability, and metal chelators are not routinely included as standard swarm-plate inhibitors in the most widely cited protocols; the claim therefore feels overstated. Finally, V does not hold because two washes reliably strip away the extracellular pyocyanin/pyoverdine, leaving a pale rather than blue-green pellet even after concentration.

Colleagues 3 and 5 correctly accept I yet reject III on the grounds that glycerol is “not characteristic”; however, that denial is weaker than the positive reports of glycerol-supported swarming that support III. Thus only I and III remain unequivocally true across the statements.}

