PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues 1–3 and 5 make a compelling case for M on the basis of published BM2/M8-type recipes and chelator studies, yet the literature on metal chelators remains more conditional than those summaries suggest. While EDTA or bipyridyl can reduce swarming under iron-limited conditions, the effect is readily rescued by modest cation supplementation and is absent or modest in many standard swarm-plate formulations; most widely cited protocols therefore do not list chelators as routine inhibitors. This variability leaves IV too unreliable to treat as definitively true.  

By contrast, the evidence for I (stab inoculation to the agar–plastic interface) and III (glycerol-supported swarming on minimal media) is unambiguous across multiple independent reports. Statements II and V continue to fail for the reasons already noted: twitching plates use only 10–15 ml to produce the required thin layer, and two washes remove pyocyanin/pyoverdine, yielding a pale pellet.  

Thus the combination that survives scrutiny most cleanly is still N (I and III). If further citations on chelator dose–response curves under standard (non-iron-starved) conditions become available they could reopen IV, but on present evidence N appears the safer choice.}

